{{short description|Orchestral piece by Edvard Grieg}}
{{About|the orchestral piece}}
[[File:(Edvard Grieg portrait) (3469853267).jpg|thumb|[[Edvard Grieg]] ca. 1870]]
"'''In the Hall of the Mountain King'''" ({{langx|no|"I Dovregubbens hall"||In the hall of the old man of [[Dovrefjell|Dovre]]|link=no|italic=no}}) is a piece of orchestral music composed by [[Edvard Grieg]] in 1875 as [[incidental music]] for the sixth scene of act 2 in [[Henrik Ibsen]]'s 1867 play ''[[Peer Gynt]]''. It was originally part of [[Peer Gynt (Grieg)|Opus 23]] but was later extracted as the final piece of [[Peer Gynt, Suite No. 1|''Peer Gynt'', Suite No. 1]], Op. 46. Its easily recognizable [[Theme (music)|theme]] has helped it attain iconic status in popular culture,<ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-10-28 |title=What's That Quirky, Spooky Music I Always Hear In Movies? {{!}} How to Classical |url=https://www.wqxr.org/story/quirky-spooky-music-movies-in-the-hall-of-the-mountain-king/ |website= wqxr.org |publisher= [[WQXR-FM|WQXR]] |language=en |access-date=2024-08-22}}</ref> where it has been [[Arrangement|arranged]] by many artists (see [[Edvard Grieg's music in popular culture|Grieg's music in popular culture]]).

The English translation of the name is not literal. [[Dovrefjell#Name|Dovre]] is a mountainous region in Norway, and {{lang|no|gubbe}} translates into "(old) man" or "husband". {{lang|no|Gubbe}} is used along with its female counterpart {{lang|no|kjerring}} to differentiate male and female [[troll]]s, {{lang|no|trollgubbe}} and {{lang|no|trollkjerring}}. In the play, Dovregubben is a troll king that Peer Gynt invents in a fantasy.
